{
"Anyone can break it when it suits him.": "a91fbd142a49",
"Chinese philosophers reasoned about the origin of order in the same way nearly two thousand years earlier. Xunzi asks where ritual and rules come from, and answers in a sentence that could be Hobbess: People are born with desires. When they desire and do not get, they cannot but seek. When they seek without measure or limit, they cannot but contend. Contention brings disorder, and disorder brings exhaustion. The former kings hated this disorder, and so they established ritual and rightness to make divisions. His account of what rules achieve is more generous than Hobbess. Rules exist, he says, to nourish desires and to see that goods and wants keep pace with each other. He also explains what rules make possible. A human being is weaker than an ox and slower than a horse, and yet oxen and horses work for us. Why? Because people can form groups and they cannot. Groups are possible because of divisions, and divisions because of a shared sense of what is right.": "8bbe062c9d7e",
"Cooperation decays when free riding goes unpunished.": "46bd411b0d4e",
"Each has reason to strike first.": "89cd550e04a6",
"From what does ritual arise? I say: people are born with desires. When they desire and do not get, they cannot but seek. When they seek without measure or limit, they cannot but contend. Contention brings disorder, and disorder brings exhaustion. The former kings hated this disorder, and so they established ritual and rightness to make divisions, in order to nourish peoples desires and to supply what they seek.": "acd2290d4a9d",
"Goods and wants keep pace with each other.": "fb83d7be542e",
"Hereby it is manifest, that during the time men live without a common Power to keep them all in awe, they are in that condition which is called Warre; and such a warre, as is of every man, against every man. For WARRE, consisteth not in Battell onely, or the act of fighting; but in a tract of time, wherein the Will to contend by Battell is sufficiently known.": "cca9fe5c390e",
"History has not borne out his answer.": "62adce1ac216",
"Hobbes concluded that only a single overwhelming power can end the trap, and here the evidence has gone against him. The political scientist Elinor Ostrom spent her career studying communities that share a resource: Swiss villages with common alpine meadows, Japanese mountain forests, Spanish irrigation canals, lobster fisheries in Maine. Many of them have managed without ruin for centuries, and they have done it with rules that the users made themselves, watched over by one another, with small penalties that grow for repeated offenses. She received the Nobel Prize in economics in 2009. Experiments tell the same story. In laboratory games, cooperation among strangers decays when free riding goes unpunished and recovers as soon as players are allowed to fine one another, which many will do even at a cost to themselves.": "8769c247b52c",
"In the twentieth century the argument was given a precise form. Two suspects are questioned separately. If both stay silent, each serves one year. If one informs and the other does not, the informer goes free and the other serves ten. If both inform, each serves five. Whatever the other does, each does better by informing, and so both inform and serve five years when they could have served one. This is the prisoners dilemma. Its lesson is that people who each choose sensibly can arrive together at a result that none of them wants. Herdsmen who each add one more animal to a shared pasture destroy it. Nations that each catch a few more fish empty the sea. The ecologist Garrett Hardin called this the tragedy of the commons and wrote, Freedom in a commons brings ruin to all.": "86eab1cccc10",
"John Locke, writing forty years later, thought Hobbes had described the state of nature at its worst and called it the whole. Even without government, he argued, people are bound by a moral law that reason can discover. The state of nature is a state of liberty, yet it is not a state of licence. No one may destroy anothers life, health, liberty, or possessions, and most people know it. What is missing is a way of settling disputes. Each person must be judge in his own case, and people are partial to themselves and carried too far by anger when they punish. Want of a common judge with authority, puts all men in a state of nature. Government is set up to supply the judge. Its purpose is to protect rights that people already had, and Locke draws the consequence that Hobbes refused. A government that attacks the rights it was made to protect has broken the agreement, and the people may replace it.": "8d26cc2e7e44",
"Mozi locates the trouble somewhere else. In the beginning, before there were rulers, he says, each person had his own view of what is right. One person, one view. Two people, two views. Ten people, ten views. Each approved his own and condemned the others, families broke apart, and the world was in chaos like that of birds and beasts. The cause of disorder, on his account, is that people sincerely disagree about what ought to be done and have no way of settling it. His remedy is a ruler chosen for his worth, whose judgment unifies the standards of all. Lockes missing judge is here already, and so is the danger that Mozi did not see, that the one who unifies the standards may be wrong.": "51e21c1ccb0d",
"No one can count on keeping what he makes.": "43d8b131802d",
"No one is in a position to make anyone keep his word.": "31f90904e05d",
"People are partial to themselves.": "8d5ba84345aa",
"Philosophers have a method for finding out what a thing is for. They imagine the world without it. Applied to government, the method gives the thought experiment known as the state of nature: human beings as they are, with their needs, fears, and abilities, and no authority over them. There are no police, courts, or laws, and no one is in a position to make anyone keep his word. The question is what life would be like, and the answers have differed so widely that they define the main positions in political thought. The purpose is to understand the present. No one who uses the method supposes that people could return to such a state.": "5f0eef5ced5d",
"Power can be divided and held to account.": "62d6f3cb2ab3",
"So the old question has a more exact answer than it had. We need rules because sincere and reasonable people, left to themselves, fall into contests and disagreements that leave all of them worse off. We need the rules to be enforced, because a good rule can be undone by a few who ignore it. And we have some choice about who does the enforcing, which is where the next three lessons begin. A philosopher of the Legalist school, Han Fei, drew the harshest conclusion from the same facts. The people submit to power, and few can be moved by rightness, he wrote, and he advised rulers to rely on rewards and punishments alone. The state that followed his advice unified China and lasted fifteen years.": "1b597e3e97a7",
"The Buddhas own community was governed in a way that Ostrom would have recognized. The monastic rules were laid down one at a time, each in response to a particular problem, and the texts record the occasion for every one. The community met regularly, decided matters by agreement of all present, and had graded penalties ending in expulsion. There was no head with power over the rest. The Buddha declined to appoint a successor and told the monks that the teaching and the discipline would be their teacher (Dharma and the West, D18).": "49e2608c22a6",
"The Buddhist canon contains a state-of-nature story of its own, told as a myth and with some humor. In the beginning, it says, beings lived on food that grew by itself. Then some began to hoard. So they said, Wed better divide up the rice and lay down boundaries. With boundaries came theft, then accusation, lying, and blows. The beings gathered and said: Why dont we elect one being who would rightly accuse those who deserve it, blame those who deserve it, and expel those who deserve it? In return they would give him a share of rice. He was called Mahāsammata, which the discourse explains as elected by the populace. The story has greed leading to property, property to conflict, and conflict to a ruler chosen by agreement and paid for a service. It places the origin of kingship in a contract and gives it no divine sanction, which was a radical thing to say in ancient India.": "d810bdd13b1f",
"The way out is an agreement. Each person gives up his right to do whatever he judges necessary, on condition that the others do the same. But an agreement of that kind is worth nothing if anyone can break it when it suits him. Covenants, without the Sword, are but Words, and of no strength to secure a man at all. So the parties must also set up a power strong enough to punish any of them, a sovereign whom Hobbes calls the Leviathan and that Mortall God. His conclusion is that almost any government is better than none, and that to resist the sovereign is to risk the return of the war.": "72d219a6bd35",
"The weakest can kill the strongest by stealth.": "318105e614f3",
"This is why Hobbes insisted on the sword. A rule that everyone would benefit from will still be broken if each person gains by breaking it while the others comply. Rules need enforcement, and the game below lets you watch that happen.": "81bcf86264d8",
"Thomas Hobbes published Leviathan in 1651, two years after the English had beheaded their king, and his account is the darkest. He starts from three facts. People are roughly equal, in that the weakest can kill the strongest by stealth or in company. They want many of the same scarce things. And none can be sure of the others intentions. It follows that each has reason to strike first, even if he wishes only to be safe, and each knows that the others have the same reason. During the time men live without a common Power to keep them all in awe, they are in that condition which is called Warre; and such a warre, as is of every man, against every man. War in his sense need not be fighting. It is the known readiness to fight, as bad weather is a tendency to rain. In that condition there is no farming, trade, building, or learning, since no one can count on keeping what he makes.": "286d09d52efb",
